       System Overview & Portfolio Context

       [0001] ClaudiaTube (internally structured as LocalTube) is a self-hosted, local-first video streaming, offline
       media archival, and personal media library platform built in Python using FastAPI and SQLite. Designed as a
       modular component of the Claudia OS ecosystem, the system unifies remote video discovery, ad-free direct
       stream proxying, concurrent batch downloading, local movie and television series indexing, and creator channel
       hosting under a single lightweight web application.


       Field of the System

       [0002] The architecture relates generally to self-hosted media servers, asynchronous task schedulers, and personal
       content management systems, and more specifically to a unified FastAPI backend that bridges cloud video
       extraction pipelines with local filesystem media libraries and Tailscale reverse-proxy identity management.


       Problem Statement & Architectural Motivation

       [0003] Cloud video platforms fragment user watch history, subscriptions, and playlists behind proprietary
       recommendation algorithms, intrusive advertisements, and recurring subscription paywalls. Meanwhile, self-
       hosted media servers (such as Jellyfin or Plex) manage local movie and television files well, but lack integrated
       cloud video search, playlist ingestion, and on-demand offline audio/video archival.

       [0004] ClaudiaTube addresses this gap by combining a zero-dependency SQLite state store, a multi-client yt-
       dlp extraction layer with automated browser cookie synchronization, a multi-threaded background download
       queue, and a regex-driven local media scanner into one cohesive service accessible over local networks or
       encrypted Tailscale mesh nodes.


       Summary of System Architecture

       [0005] The backend comprises five primary subsystems: (1) a reverse-proxy identity resolver supporting Tailscale
       and forwarded user headers; (2) a multi-tab search, pagination, and 15-minute TTL direct-stream URL cache; (3)
       a background subscription feed scanner polling every 300 seconds into a persistent SQLite page_cache; (4) a
       concurrent download worker daemon with real-time speed/ETA progress hooks and FFmpeg video/audio




