       Project Overview

       [0001] After Hours TV is a custom Electron desktop television and channel-guide application built by Don
       Semsey. Designed to run in a fullscreen window while supporting an electronic program guide (Prevue-style
       guide view), control room / admin settings, embedded RetroCast weather view, and a browser-based LAN phone
       remote, the project brings together local media libraries, online video sources, automated video/audio analysis,
       TV metadata matching, and live channel state persistence.


       Purpose & Problem Addressed

       [0002] Standard media players and streaming apps treat videos as isolated on-demand files rather than parts of
       curated television channels with scheduled blocks, commercials, bumpers, guide listings, and companion remote
       control across local devices. In addition, organizing mixed local folders alongside online streams (such as
       LocalTube, yt-dlp sources, Archive.org items, and direct web video links) typically requires manual metadata
       cleanup and uneven volume levels.

       [0003] After Hours TV addresses these needs inside a single desktop runtime by combining channel and library
       configuration (network.json), live playback state tracking (live-state.json), background FFmpeg
       analysis for black frames and loudness normalization, TheTVDB v4 metadata lookup with encrypted credential
       storage, and a PIN-paired HTTP phone remote on port 8765.


       Summary of System Architecture

       [0004]    The application centers on an Electron main process (main.js) paired with a sandboxed
       contextBridge preload script (preload.js) exposing the window.tv API to the renderer (renderer/
       index.html). Key architectural subsystems include: (1) channel, media, and live-state storage; (2) local folder/
       file and online/direct-page media importers; (3) FFmpeg blackdetect and loudnorm analysis; (4)
       TheTVDB v4 episode/movie/series matching protected by Electron safeStorage; (5) an embedded RetroCast
       WebContentsView targeting https://weather.com/retro/ with scanline and vignette CSS plus
       automatic audio tuning; and (6) an HTTP LAN remote server serving renderer/mobile-remote.html




