       System Overview & Portfolio Context

       [0001] The Retro 1990s/2000s Desktop & Internet Simulator is a self-contained, kiosk-mode desktop operating
       environment and closed-loop historical web ecosystem built on Electron and Chromium. Rather than emulating
       hardware at the CPU instruction level, the platform reconstructs the visual, auditory, and interactive experience of
       late-1990s and early-2000s personal computing—uniting a custom Windows XP desktop shell, a ground-up
       HTML/CSS/JavaScript recreation of the NeoPlanet web browser, an internal closed-loop retro email network, and
       a wall-clock-synchronized 1990s television broadcast simulator.


       Field of the System

       [0002] The architecture relates generally to desktop environment simulation, historical software preservation, and
       algorithmic media scheduling, and more particularly to a kiosk-locked web runtime that integrates extracted
       legacy executable resources, sandboxed intranet routing, scripted electronic mail narratives, and deterministic
       modulo-time video playback.


       Problem Statement & Architectural Motivation

       [0003] Traditional virtual machines running legacy operating systems suffer from high setup friction, broken
       external web links due to deprecated TLS/SSL ciphers, dead third-party web services, and uncurated modern
       internet exposure. Conversely, static nostalgia websites capture only isolated pages without the surrounding
       operating system shell, dial-up connection cadence, desktop messaging, or ambient living-room television
       environment.

       [0004] This simulator solves both limitations by enclosing the entire experience inside a fullscreen kiosk
       Electron/Chromium runtime. All web portals, skinnable browser chrome, email exchanges, and multi-channel
       television broadcasts operate coherently inside a deterministic, curated time capsule.


       Summary of System Architecture




