       Detailed Technical Specification

       1. Kiosk Electron/Chromium Host & Windows XP Desktop Shell

       [0007] At launch, the Electron main process initializes a borderless, kiosk-locked Chromium browser window
       that suppresses host OS window decorations and captures system shortcuts. Inside the viewport, a modular DOM/
       CSS window manager renders classic Windows XP Luna visual styles, draggable/resizable application windows,
       cascading Start menu hierarchies, active taskbar buttons, and a live system tray clock synchronized to the
       simulated era.

       [0008]   A simulated dial-up networking module precedes internet session activity with authentic modem
       handshake audio, connection status dialogs, and optional 56 kbps bandwidth throttling that restores progressive
       scanline image loading across web pages.

       2. NeoPlanet Browser Recreation & PE Resource Extraction

       [0009] The centerpiece web client is a ground-up HTML, CSS, and JavaScript recreation of the late-1990s
       NeoPlanet browser. Authentic button states, toolbar textures, animated throbbers, and icons are sourced from
       legacy Portable Executable resource sections (.rsrc) and archived ThemeWorld skin packages, allowing
       dynamic runtime theme switching without reloading the underlying web viewport.




ORIGINAL TECHNICAL SOURCE · PORTFOLIO APPENDIX                                                                APPENDIX / 4

       [0010] Navigation requests are intercepted by a closed-loop protocol router that maps historical URLs, directory
       categories, and NeoPlanet Channel Bar links to a local curated archive of late-1990s portals, personal homepages,
       WebRings, MIDI jukeboxes, and interactive guestbooks.

       3. Closed-Loop Retro Email & Narrative Event Bus

       [0011] Integrated alongside the browser is a standalone closed-loop retro email client featuring Inbox, Outbox,
       Sent, Drafts, and Address Book panes backed by persistent local storage. Because the simulator operates without
       live external SMTP/POP3 exposure, outbound messages pass through an internal message router that simulates
       send/receive queue latency and triggers classic audio notifications.

       [0012] An event-driven reply engine inspects recipient addresses and message keywords to dispatch time-delayed
       responses from scripted 1990s personas (such as webmasters, ISP tech support, and newsletter bots). Incoming
       emails can attach virtual files, unlock hidden intranet URLs, or install new desktop wallpapers and NeoPlanet
       skins into the user's virtual filesystem.

       4. Algorithmic 1990s TV Scheduler & Broadcast Sync

       [0013] Accessible either as a resizable Picture-in-Picture tuner window on the XP desktop or in fullscreen living-
       room CRT mode, the 1990s TV simulator recreates multi-channel cable and broadcast programming. Channels are
       organized by era programming blocks—including Saturday morning cartoons, prime-time sitcoms, music video
       countdowns, late-night tech shows, and an auto-scrolling Prevue-style electronic program guide.

       [0014] Rather than starting every video from timestamp 00:00 upon channel selection, a deterministic modulo
       scheduler computes the exact elapsed offset within the current wall-clock broadcast slot (offset = (T_now
       - T_slot) mod L) and instructs the YouTube IFrame API (or local media player) to seek directly into the
       active program or stitched 1990s commercial pod, complete with channel-surf static bursts, green phosphor On-
       Screen Display (OSD) overlays, and CRT scanline shaders.

