   FIELD OF THE INVENTION

   [0002] The present invention relates generally to emergency medical services (EMS) and public safety
   communications, and more particularly to an operator-owned, decentralized sub-GHz radio mesh network
   that provides zero-connectivity triage data synchronization and patient tracking without relying on
   commercial cellular infrastructure or cloud backends.


   BACKGROUND OF THE INVENTION AND PRIOR ART

   [0003] During mass-casualty events, natural disasters, or infrastructural collapse, commercial cellular
   networks saturate, fail, or lose power instantly. Conventional emergency medical record tools depend
   entirely on centralized cloud servers and continuous cellular data connectivity.

   [0004] When cellular networks fail, emergency medical personnel are forced to fall back on analog paper
   triage tags, resulting in severe tracking blind spots, coordination delays, and increased patient mortality
   during the critical response window. Existing digital solutions fail to operate offline because they lack
   native peer-to-peer radio architectures, forcing reliance on vulnerable telecom service providers.

   [0005] Accordingly, there is a critical need for an always-on, default-mesh emergency medical
   communication system that utilizes license-free sub-GHz frequencies and mobile vehicle relay nodes to
   guarantee continuous patient tracking and triage coordination under zero-infrastructure conditions.


   SUMMARY OF THE INVENTION

   [0006] The present invention overcomes the deficiencies of the prior art by providing a decentralized, sub-
   GHz packet radio mesh network where medical tablets and field terminals communicate peer-to-peer



