   [0009] Reference will now be made in detail to preferred embodiments of the invention. The emergency
   triage communication system comprises three primary subsystems: (1) The Sub-GHz Packet Radio Field
   Terminal, (2) The Mobile Vehicle Relay Backbone, and (3) The Multi-Tier Fallback Synchronization
   Engine.

   1. SUB-GHZ PACKET RADIO FIELD TERMINAL

   [0010] Field units comprise ruggedized computing tablets integrated with license-free sub-GHz radio
   transceiver modules (operating within the 915 MHz or 433 MHz ISM bands). Patient triage data entered
   into the local software interface is serialized into lightweight, compressed binary packets designed for
   rapid RF transmission.

   [0011] Transmission range and packet integrity across obstructed urban environments are maintained via
   automatic multi-hop packet forwarding. Each node in the network acts as a router, rebroadcasting packets
   to extend operational reach without infrastructure dependency.

   2. MOBILE VEHICLE RELAY BACKBONE

   [0012] To overcome urban signal attenuation caused by concrete structures, public safety vehicles (police
   cruisers and ambulances) are equipped with high-gain roof-mounted omnidirectional antennas and high-
   capacity secondary power systems.

   [0013] These vehicles operate as mobile routing nodes, creating a high-power infrastructure canopy over
   disaster zones. The network routing protocol dynamically optimizes packet paths across stationary and
   mobile nodes to ensure delivery to command units and hospital triage stations.

   3. MATHEMATICAL PACKET SYNCHRONIZATION & FALLBACK LOGIC

   [0014] Patient record synchronization across intermittent nodes is managed via a decentralized vector
   clock conflict-resolution algorithm. The synchronization state $S_{sync}$ for patient record $p_i$ across
   nodes $N$ is defined as:


      S_{sync}(p_i) = \max_{j \in N} \left( Timestamp(p_{i,j}),
      Version(p_{i,j}) \right)


   [0015] Network priority routing logic $R_{route}$ evaluates connectivity state vectors to determine
   transmission pathways:

      R_{route} = \begin{cases} \text{Sub-GHz Mesh}, & \text{if } Link_{mesh}
      \geq \text{Threshold} \\ \text{Cellular LTE/5G}, & \text{if }
      Link_{mesh} < \text{Threshold} \text{ and } Status_{cell} ==
      \text{Active} \\ \text{Paper Tag Fallback}, & \text{if Hardware / Power
      Failure} \end{cases}


