           BACKGROUND OF THE INVENTION


                   [0003] High-speed vehicle pursuits present severe risks to public safety, property, and
           law enforcement personnel. Traditional pursuit methods require police cruisers to maintain
           close proximity at high speeds, frequently escalating suspect recklessness and leading to
           catastrophic accidents. While stationary rooftop drone docking stations ("Drone as First
           Responder" systems) offer aerial tracking solutions, their operational range is strictly limited
           to the fixed radius of the docking station.

                   [0004] Furthermore, existing attempts to mount unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs) on
           moving vehicles suffer from extreme aerodynamic turbulence during transit, complex manual
           recovery challenges, and severe battery endurance limitations that terminate tracking
           capabilities prematurely. Accordingly, there is a critical need for a mobile, self-contained,
           aerodynamically shielded multi-drone launching and automated docking system that travels
           with the pursuit unit to provide indefinite aerial surveillance.


           SUMMARY OF THE INVENTION


                   [0005] The present invention addresses the aforementioned limitations by providing a
           vehicle-integrated multi-drone system comprising a dual-shell roof structure, retractable
           access panels, deployable internal wind-break shields, a plurality of autonomous tactical


